{{Short description|Physical separation process}}
{{Other uses|Strip (disambiguation){{!}}Strip}}
'''Stripping''' is a physical [[separation process]] where one or more components are removed from a liquid stream by a vapor stream.<ref>{{Cite book|last=Warren L.|first=McCabe|title=UNIT OPERATIONS OF CHEMICAL ENGINEERING|last2=Julian C.|first2=Smith|last3=Peter|first3=Harriott|publisher=McGraw Hill|isbn=0-07-112738-0|edition=5|pages=686}}</ref> In industrial applications the liquid and vapor streams can have co-current or countercurrent flows. Stripping is usually carried out in either a packed or trayed column.<ref name=Seader>{{cite book|author=J.D. Seader and E.J. Henley|title=Separation Process Principles|edition=2nd|publisher=John Wiley & Sons|year=2006|isbn=0-471-46480-5}}</ref>

{{Short description|Using digital media for furthering historical analysis, presentation, and research}}

'''Digital history''' is the use of [[digital media]] to further [[history|historical]] analysis, presentation, and research. It is a branch of the [[digital humanities]] and an extension of [[quantitative history]], [[cliometrics]], and computing. Digital history is commonly known as digital public history, concerned primarily with engaging online audiences with historical content, or digital research methods, that further academic research. Digital history outputs include: digital [[archive]]s, online presentations, [[data and information visualization]]s, interactive maps, [[timeline]]s, audio files, and [[virtual world]]s.
These outputs are designed to enhance accessibility to users, facilitating engagement with historical content. Recent digital history projects focus on creativity, collaboration, and technical innovation, [[text mining]], [[corpus linguistics]], [[social network analysis|network analysis]], [[3D modeling]], and [[big data]] analysis. By utilizing these resources, the user can rapidly develop new analyses that can link to, extend, and bring to life existing histories.

